```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("setup.R")
vp_setup("features-hierarchy")
vp_manual_data()
```

```{r models, include = FALSE}
vp_manual_models()
```

# Measuring each feature {#sec:features}

## Every measure of every feature {#sec:featuretable}

*Function:* `vp_feature_table()`

**The question.** Which features does everything else depend on? Which ones depend on many others? Which ones decide the most?

`vp_feature_table()` gives one row per feature that can vary, with one column per measure. Each measure is given in words, with an example, and as a formula, in the notation of this box:

::: {.vp-box .vp-goodtoknow}
**Notation of the formulas.** $V$ is the set of features that can vary (the nodes of the graphs) and $n = |V|$ their number; $|X|$ is the number of elements of a set $X$. For two features $f$ and $g$ of $V$:

-   $f \Rightarrow g$: $f$ requires $g$ (an arrow of the requires graph);

-   $f \mathbin{\#} g$: $f$ excludes $g$ (a line of the excludes graph);

-   $f \vee g$: every product has $f$ or $g$ (a line of the at-least-one graph);

-   $f \to g$: an arrow of the skeleton $S$, the requires graph without the arrows that follow from others ([The skeleton](#sec:skeleton)), and $d_S(f, g)$ the number of arrows of the shortest chain of $S$ from $f$ to $g$ ($\infty$ when there is none).

:::

The table has fifteen columns, so here they are a few at a time, picked with `select()`. Each group comes with a table that gives its formula and a one-line meaning, followed by a detailed explanation of every column on the drawings of the band.

### Four counts

```{r ft_counts}
ft <- vp_feature_table(band)
ft |> select(feature, required_by, requires, excludes, atleastone)
```

| **Column**   | **Formula**                        | **Meaning**                                                  |
|:-------------|:-----------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------|
| `required_by` | $|\{g \in V : g \Rightarrow f\}|$  | How many features require this one (its sensitivity).        |
| `requires` | $|\{g \in V : f \Rightarrow g\}|$  | How many features it requires.                               |
| `excludes` | $|\{g \in V : f \mathbin{\#} g\}|$ | How many features it excludes (its conflict degree).         |
| `atleastone` | $|\{g \in V : f \vee g\}|$         | How many features it is joined to in the at-least-one graph. |

The figure is the requires graph of the band, and the figure its excludes graph; the links of *MovementFilter* are thick. Every count of this group can be read on them.

![The requires graph of the Mi Band 3. The four thick arrows leave *MovementFilter*: selecting it selects *BLEv40* and the three features of the atomic set of the screen (dashed box).](figures/am_power_requires.svg){#fig:powerreq}

![The excludes graph of the Mi Band 3. The three thick lines join *MovementFilter* to the features it excludes: *HeartRateSensor*, *BLEv42* and *BT40*.](figures/am_power_excludes.svg){#fig:powerexc}

**`required_by`: how many features depend on this one.** Count the arrows that *arrive* at the feature in the figure. Six arrive at *Screen*: from *TouchScreen*, *MovementFilter*, *BLEv40*, *BLEv42*, and from *OLED* and *CapacityButton*, the other two members of its atomic set. Those six features form the **impact set** of the screen: if the screen is removed, renamed or given a new requirement, all six are affected [Abate et al. (2009)](#ref-Abate09). The measure is also called the **sensitivity** of a feature. Because the requires graph is complete, the count includes the features that depend on it through a chain: *MovementFilter* reaches the screen only through *BLEv40*, and it is counted. *Use it* to find the features that deserve the most careful review and the most testing: a mistake in them spreads to many others.

**`requires`: how many features this one depends on.** Count the arrows that *leave* the feature. Four leave *MovementFilter*: *BLEv40*, *Screen*, *OLED* and *CapacityButton*. So selecting the movement filter brings four features along, and four features can break it: if any of them changes, the movement filter may stop working. *Use it* to find fragile features, and the features that a user cannot select without accepting many others.

**`excludes`: how many features this one rules out.** Count the lines of the feature in the figure: three for *MovementFilter*, seven for *BT40*. It is the **conflict degree** of the feature: what selecting it costs in lost choices. A feature that excludes almost everything is available only in a small corner of the products, which is worth checking against the intention of whoever wrote it.

**`atleastone`: how many features leaving this one out forces in.** Count the lines of the feature in the at-least-one graph (the figure): *BT40* has three, to the three features of the atomic set of the screen, because every band has a screen or classic Bluetooth. It is `NA` when the run had no `or_graph = TRUE`. *Why it matters*: these are the features a user cannot simply refuse. In a configurator, leaving out one of them forces another in, so a high count marks a feature whose "no" has consequences that users do not expect; for testing, each line removes one combination ("neither") that no product can have.

![The at-least-one graph of the Mi Band 3. A dotted green line joins two features of which every band has at least one.](figures/am_atleastone.svg){#fig:alo}

### What a decision settles

```{r ft_power}
ft |> select(feature, decision_power, decision_power_off, isolated)
```

| **Column**   | **Formula**                                                                       | **Meaning**                                                  |
|:-------------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------|
| `decision_power` | $\dfrac{\text{requires}(f) + \text{excludes}(f)}{n - 1}$                          | The share of the other features that selecting it decides.   |
| `decision_power_off` | $\dfrac{\text{required\_by}(f) + \text{atleastone}(f)}{n - 1}$                    | The share of the other features that leaving it out decides. |
| `isolated` | $\text{required\_by} + \text{requires} + \text{excludes} + \text{atleastone} = 0$ | `TRUE` for a feature in no graph.                      |

**`decision_power`: what selecting the feature settles.** Selecting the movement filter settles 4 features by the arrows of the figure (they are selected too) and 3 by the lines of the figure (they cannot be selected). Of the other 10 features of the band, 7 are decided: the decision power is $(4 + 3)/10 = 0.7$. Selecting *NFC* settles nothing: it has no arrow leaving it and no line, so its decision power is 0. The measure says how much a *yes* settles; averaged over all features (`mean_decision_power`, [The whole model in numbers](#sec:model)) it says how fast a configuration session collapses. It is not, by itself, the best order in which to ask the questions, because the answer can be *no* (see the box "Decision power is not the order in which to ask" below). Mann and Rock define the same ratio on the written model [Mann and Rock (2011)](#ref-MannRock11).

**`decision_power_off`: what leaving the feature out settles.** Leaving a feature out works the other way round. Every feature whose arrow *arrives* at it must be left out too (it could not have it), and every feature joined to it by an at-least-one line must be selected. Leaving out *Screen* rules out the 6 features whose arrows arrive at it in the figure and forces *BT40* in, by the line of the figure: $(6 + 1)/10 = 0.7$. Leaving out *NFC* rules out *ContactlessPayment*, which requires it: $1/10 = 0.1$. It is `NA` without `or_graph = TRUE`. *Why it matters*: in a configuration session "no" is as frequent as "yes", often more. A feature whose refusal settles much (the screen, here) is a feature whose removal from a product, or from the product line, reaches many others: a high value marks the features that cannot be dropped lightly.

**`isolated`: is the feature in no graph?** `TRUE` when the feature has no arrow and no line at all. No feature of the band is isolated; BusyBox has 38. As far as relationships between two features go, such a feature is free. Some are truly independent; others are constrained only by rules over three or more features, which the graphs do not show; a few may be left-overs that nothing uses any more. *Why it matters*: isolated features are the cheapest to test (they can be combined freely), the first suspects in a clean-up of dead options, and, when many, a hint that the model holds constraints the graphs cannot see.

### Measures on the skeleton

The last columns look at the skeleton only (the figure): the arrows that do not follow from others, close to what was written. On the skeleton, each arrow is one step, so it makes sense to talk about how far apart two features are, which in the complete graph, where every requirement is a direct arrow, would always be one step.

![The skeleton of the Mi Band 3 (the atomic set of the screen is one node). The thick arrows are the only chain of two steps; *BLEv40*, highlighted, is the only feature between two others.](figures/am_betweenness.svg){#fig:betweenness}

```{r ft_skeleton}
ft |> select(feature, level, direct_required_by, direct_requires)
ft |> select(feature, betweenness, closeness_out, closeness_in, harmonic)
ft |> filter(!is.na(atomic_set)) |> select(feature, atomic_set)
```

| **Column**   | **Formula**                                                                                                                                                                                      | **Meaning**                                                 |
|:-------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------|
| `level` | $0$ if $f$ has no arrow in $S$; else $1 + \max_{f \to g} \text{level}(g)$                                                                                                                        | Its layer in the hierarchy.                                 |
| `direct_required_by` | $|\{g : g \to f\}|$                                                                                                                                                                              | The features that require it directly.                      |
| `direct_requires` | $|\{g : f \to g\}|$                                                                                                                                                                              | The features it requires directly.                          |
| `betweenness` | $\displaystyle\sum_{s \neq f \neq t} \frac{\sigma_{st}(f)}{\sigma_{st}}$, where $\sigma_{st}$ counts the shortest chains of $S$ from $s$ to $t$ and $\sigma_{st}(f)$ those that pass through $f$ | How many chains of requirements pass through it.            |
| `closeness_out` | $\dfrac{k}{\sum_{g} d_S(f, g)}$, over the $k$ features $g$ that $f$ reaches                                                                                                                      | One over the average number of steps to what it requires.   |
| `closeness_in` | $\dfrac{k}{\sum_{g} d_S(g, f)}$, over the $k$ features $g$ that reach $f$                                                                                                                        | One over the average number of steps from what requires it. |
| `harmonic` | $\displaystyle\sum_{g \neq f} \frac{1}{d_S(f, g)}$, with $1/\infty = 0$                                                                                                                          | The sum of one over the steps to each requirement.          |
| `atomic_set` |                                                                                                                                                                                                  | The first feature of its atomic set, if it is in one.       |

In all of them an atomic set counts as one node, as in the figure, and its members share its values.

**`level`: the layer of the feature.** A feature that needs nothing is at level 0 (the screen, the heart-rate sensor, *BT40*, *NFC*); a feature whose direct requirements are all at level 0 is at level 1 (*TouchScreen*, *ContactlessPayment*, *BLEv40*, *BLEv42*); *MovementFilter*, which needs *BLEv40*, is at level 2. The level is the length of the longest chain of requirements that starts at the feature, so it is the number of steps of the longest explanation of why selecting it forces something. *Why it matters*: features of level 0 are the foundations, to be built and stabilised first; a high level marks a feature that rests on a tall stack of others, so it is more likely to be broken by changes below it and harder to explain to users. A configurator asks about features in the order of their levels ([A safe order for the questions](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:rounds)). [Levels, depth and the longest chain](#sec:levels) draws the levels and uses them.

**`direct_required_by` and `direct_requires`: the arrows of the skeleton.** Count the arrows that arrive at, or leave, the feature in the figure. Three arrive at the screen, from *TouchScreen*, *BLEv40* and *BLEv42*, against six in the complete graph: the other three are *MovementFilter*, which reaches the screen only through *BLEv40*, and *OLED* and *CapacityButton*, which are the screen itself as far as choices go. *BLEv42* has two direct requirements, the screen and the heart-rate sensor. The difference between a count and its direct version is what the chains add: a feature with many dependants but few direct ones is a hub by inheritance, which `vp_genuine_hubs()` (below) examines.

**`betweenness`: is the feature a gateway?** Some features are not the beginning of a requirement, nor its end, but a step in the middle: a feature $f$ requires a feature $g$ only *through* them. Betweenness counts how often a feature is such a step. It is measured on the skeleton, where each arrow is one step, in three steps:

1.  Take every pair of features $s$ and $t$ such that $s$ requires $t$ through a chain of two arrows or more of the skeleton. (A direct arrow has nothing in between, so it gives no point to anybody.)

2.  Find the shortest chain from $s$ to $t$.

3.  Give one point to every feature in the middle of that chain. If several chains are equally short, share the point among them: with two such chains, each feature in the middle of one of them gets half a point.

The betweenness of a feature is the sum of its points: in the formula of the table, $\sigma_{st}$ is the number of shortest chains from $s$ to $t$, and $\sigma_{st}(f)$ the number of those that pass through $f$.

*On the band.* The pairs of step 1 are the arrows of the requires graph that are not in the skeleton, between different nodes: those that need a chain. `vp_implied_requires()` ([The skeleton](#sec:skeleton)) lists them, and `vp_explain_requires()` gives the chain:

```{r betweenness_band}
vp_implied_requires(band)
vp_explain_requires(band, "MovementFilter", "Screen") |> filter(route == 1)
```

There is a single pair, and a single chain: *MovementFilter* → *BLEv40* → the screen (the thick arrows of the figure). *BLEv40* is in its middle, so it gets the only point: its betweenness is 1, and every other feature has 0. The screen is the most required feature of the band, yet its betweenness is 0: it *ends* chains, it does not relay them. A feature with a high betweenness is a **gateway**: the point through which indirect requirements pass, and where implicit dependencies come from.

*On a bigger band.* The Mi Band 8, the last generation of the history of [Reading the results](reading-and-drawing.html#sec:read), has longer chains:

```{r betweenness_band8}
vp_feature_table(history$model[[7]]) |>
  filter(betweenness > 0, is.na(atomic_set) | atomic_set == feature) |>
  select(feature, betweenness, required_by) |>
  arrange(desc(betweenness))
```

Being required and being a gateway are different things. Compare two features of the table above:

```{r betweenness_compare}
vp_feature_table(history$model[[7]]) |>
  filter(feature %in% c("AMOLED", "HeartRateSensor")) |>
  select(feature, required_by, betweenness)
```


The heart-rate sensor is required by more features than the AMOLED screen (`r facts$EightHeart` against `r facts$EightAmoled`), and yet its betweenness is 0 while the AMOLED screen's is 15. The figure shows why.

```{r betweenness8_facts, include = FALSE}
t8 <- vp_feature_table(history$model[[7]])
fact("EightHeart", t8$required_by[t8$feature == "HeartRateSensor"])
fact("EightAmoled", t8$required_by[t8$feature == "AMOLED"])
stopifnot(
  t8$betweenness[t8$feature == "HeartRateSensor"] == 0,
  t8$betweenness[t8$feature == "AMOLED"] == 15,
  t8$required_by[t8$feature == "HeartRateSensor"] >
    t8$required_by[t8$feature == "AMOLED"]
)
```

![The skeleton of the Mi Band 8, with the betweenness of each node in brackets. The AMOLED screen (yellow) relays the chains from the six features below it to the three above it, except those from *Infrared*, whose shortest way to the touch screen goes through *ContactlessPayment* (thick arrows).](figures/am_betweenness8.svg){#fig:betweenness8}

*The heart-rate sensor is at the top.* It needs nothing, so every chain that reaches it *ends* there. It is never in the middle of a chain, and gets no point.

*The AMOLED screen is in the middle* (an atomic set of five features, one node of the figure). Six features reach it by chains of arrows: *Photoplethysmogram*, *SpO2*, *Infrared*, *BLEv50*, *BLEv51* and *BLEv52*. Beyond it lie three: the touch screen, the screen and the heart-rate sensor. That makes $6 \times 3 = 18$ pairs whose chains could pass through the AMOLED screen. For three of them, those that start at *Infrared*, the shortest chain does not: *Infrared* reaches the touch screen in two steps through *ContactlessPayment* (thick arrows), and in three through the AMOLED screen. Only the shortest chain counts, so the AMOLED screen gets $18 - 3 = 15$ points. (The touch screen, just above it, gets 16: almost every chain of the band goes through it on its way to the screen or to the heart-rate sensor.)

*In short:* `required_by` says how many features depend on a feature; `betweenness` says how many dependencies travel *through* it.

*Why it matters.* Changing the heart-rate sensor affects its `r facts$EightHeart` dependants. Changing the requirements of the AMOLED screen (say, it no longer needs the touch screen) breaks 15 indirect requirements at once, of features that never mention the touch screen themselves: it rewires everything below it. So a gateway is riskier to edit than its `required_by` suggests. Menus and switches of whole subsystems typically show up as gateways. Betweenness is computed on the skeleton because in the complete requires graph every requirement is a direct arrow, every chain has one step, and every betweenness would be 0.

**`closeness_out`: how far away the requirements are.** Some features list everything they need in their own declaration; others need features that are only reached through a chain. `closeness_out` tells them apart, in four steps:

1.  In the skeleton, follow the arrows from the feature and list every node that can be reached: these are its requirements (an atomic set is one node, so it is counted once). Call their number $k$.

2.  For each node reached, count the arrows of the shortest way to it: its *distance*, $d_S$.

3.  Average the distances: add them up and divide by $k$.

4.  Closeness is 1 divided by that average distance.

Why divide 1 by the average? So that the number grows when the requirements are *closer*. An average distance of 1 (every requirement is a direct arrow) gives closeness $1/1 = 1$, the highest possible value; an average of 2 gives $1/2 = 0.5$; an average of 4 gives $1/4 = 0.25$. The farther the requirements, the smaller the closeness. (Since the average is the sum of the distances divided by $k$, dividing 1 by it is the same as dividing $k$ by the sum of the distances, which is how the formula in the table above is written: $k / \sum d_S$.)

For *MovementFilter*, in the figure:

| **Step**              |                                                                         |    **Value**     |
|:----------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------|:----------------:|
| 1\. Nodes reached     | *BLEv40*; the screen (with *OLED* and *CapacityButton*, its atomic set) |     $k = 2$      |
| 2\. Distances         | *BLEv40*: 1 arrow; the screen: 2 arrows, through *BLEv40*               |     1 and 2      |
| 3\. Average distance  | (sum of the distances) / $k$ $= (1 + 2) / 2$                            |      $1.5$       |
| 4\. **closeness_out** | $1 /$ (average distance) $= 1 / 1.5$                                    | $\mathbf{0.667}$ |

The same last step, written as in the formula: $k / (\text{sum of the distances}) = 2 / 3 = 0.667$.

*BLEv42* reaches its two requirements, the screen and the heart-rate sensor, in one step each: $k = 2$, sum 2, closeness $2/2 = 1$. So does *TouchScreen*, with one requirement at one step: $1/1 = 1$. A low value marks a feature whose requirements are mostly indirect: reading its own declaration tells little about what it drags in, so it is a likely source of surprises for users. It is `NA` for a feature that requires nothing ($k = 0$), and it does not say how many requirements there are: one requirement at one step, and ten at one step each, both give 1 (`harmonic`, below, does).

**`closeness_in`: how far away the dependants are.** The same, in the other direction: the features that reach this one. The screen is reached by *TouchScreen*, *BLEv40* and *BLEv42* in one step and by *MovementFilter* in two: $4/(1 + 1 + 1 + 2) = 0.8$. A low value marks a hub whose dependants are far away: the case in which those who change the hub are least likely to know who depends on it.

**`harmonic`: how much a feature drags in, the near counting more.** `closeness_out` answers "how far away, on average, are the requirements?"; it does not care how many there are. The harmonic centrality answers a different question: "how much does selecting the feature drag in, if a direct requirement counts fully and a far one counts less?". Its first two steps are those of `closeness_out`:

1.  In the skeleton, list every node that the feature reaches (its requirements; an atomic set counts once).

2.  For each, count the arrows of the shortest way to it: its distance.

3.  Turn each distance into a weight, 1 divided by the distance: a direct requirement weighs $1/1 = 1$, one at two steps $1/2 = 0.5$, one at three steps $1/3$, and so on. A node that is not reached weighs 0.

4.  Add the weights.

For *MovementFilter*: *BLEv40* at one step weighs 1, the screen at two steps weighs 0.5; harmonic $= 1 + 0.5 = 1.5$. Three features of the band show how the two measures differ:

| **Feature**      | **Requirements**                  | **Distances** | **closeness_out** |   **harmonic**    |
|:-----------------|:----------------------------------|:--------------|:-----------------:|:-----------------:|
| *TouchScreen*    | the screen                        | 1             |     $1/1 = 1$     |     $1/1 = 1$     |
| *BLEv42*         | the screen, the heart-rate sensor | 1 and 1       |     $1/1 = 1$     |  $1/1 + 1/1 = 2$  |
| *MovementFilter* | *BLEv40*, the screen              | 1 and 2       |  $1/1.5 = 0.667$  | $1/1 + 1/2 = 1.5$ |
| *Screen*         | nothing                           |               |   `NA`    |         0         |

`closeness_out` cannot tell *TouchScreen* from *BLEv42*: both have their requirements one step away. `harmonic` can: *BLEv42* drags in twice as much. And it ranks *MovementFilter* in between: it drags in two requirements, but one of them from far.

**Which one to use.**

|                   | **`closeness_out`**                                                                                                                                                                  | **`harmonic`**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
|:------------------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Question**      | How indirect are the requirements?                                                                                                                                                | How much does the feature drag in, the near counting more?                                                                                                                                                                                                |
| **Use it to**     | find features whose requirements are hidden behind chains, whatever their number: those whose own declaration tells least about what they bring, the likely sources of surprises. | rank features by how much they pull in; compare features that have different numbers of requirements; average or sum over many features.                                                                                                                  |
| **Advantages**    | A pure measure of distance, easy to read: 1 means "everything is direct", 0.5 "two steps on average".                                                                             | Defined for every feature (0 when it requires nothing), so no row is lost in a ranking or an average. Grows with every requirement, and still gives weight to the far ones. The usual choice of network science for graphs in several pieces, like these. |
| **Disadvantages** | `NA` for features that require nothing. Blind to how many requirements there are. With one or two requirements it jumps between few values.                               | Mixes number and distance, so one value can mean different things: two requirements at one step and four at two steps both give 2. Grows with the size of the model, so compare features of the same model (divide by $n - 1$ to compare models).         |

**`atomic_set`: is the feature one of a group?** The first feature of its atomic set ([Atomic sets](#sec:atomic)), or `NA`. The three features of the band's set are always selected together, so for every measure of the skeleton they are one feature. *Why it matters*: members of an atomic set are one decision, so a configurator should ask about them once and a test plan should vary them together.

**Using it.** The table is a tibble, so the usual verbs work. The features that decide most, first:

```{r ft_arrange}
ft |>
  arrange(desc(decision_power)) |>
  select(feature, decision_power, requires, excludes) |>
  head(4)
```

::: {.vp-box .vp-careful}
**Decision power is not the order in which to ask.** It is tempting to conclude that a configurator should ask first about the features with the highest decision power. But a question has two answers. The movement filter is in only a quarter of the bands: asked about it, users answer *no* three times out of four, and a *no* settles nothing (its `decision_power_off` is 0).

Heradio et al. compared orders of configuration questions on real models (a Renault car and an e-commerce platform) and found that the fewest questions are needed when the next question is the one whose answer is the most *uncertain*: the one with the highest entropy, $H = -p \log_2 p - (1 - p) \log_2 (1 - p)$, where $p$ is the share of the valid products that have the feature [Heradio et al. (2014)](#ref-Heradio14). Asking first about the features involved in the most constraints, the closest to decision power, needed more questions. A feature with $p = 1/2$ is the best question: whatever the answer, it halves the products left.

Entropy needs $p$, and $p$ comes from counting the products (with a BDD or a #SAT solver), which the graphs cannot do. For the band, whose 24 products were enumerated for the vignettes, it can be computed:

```{r ft_entropy}
# the 24 valid bands, one per row
products <- read.csv("data/miband3_products.csv")
ft |>
  mutate(
    p = colMeans(products[feature]),
    entropy = -p * log2(p) - (1 - p) * log2(1 - p)
  ) |>
  select(feature, decision_power, decision_power_off, p, entropy) |>
  arrange(desc(entropy))
```

*BLEv40*, in half of the bands, is the best first question (entropy 1). The three features with the highest decision power, *MovementFilter*, *BLEv42* and *BT40*, come last: each is in a quarter of the bands. Decision power describes the model; to order the questions of a configurator, use the entropy, recomputed after each answer as the article explains.

:::

On a bigger model the table is where most studies start. For the last month of the BusyBox history, a chart of how many features require each feature against how many it requires shows at a glance the hubs (far right), the demanding features (high up), and the few that are both:

```{r ft_chart}
bb <- busybox$model[[nrow(busybox)]]
p <- vp_feature_table(bb) |>
  ggplot(aes(required_by, requires)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.15, height = 0.15, alpha = 0.4, colour = "#0B3D91") +
  labs(x = "required by (features)", y = "requires (features)")
```

```{r ft_chart_save, include = FALSE}
save_chart(p, "ft_chart", height = 2.6)
```

![BusyBox, `r facts$BusyLast`: one dot per feature. Most features need little and are needed by few (bottom left).](figures/am_ft_chart.svg){#fig:ftchart}

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

-   Every count is a count of relationships between two features. A feature that is constrained only by a rule over three or more features ("A needs B or C") gets nothing from that rule: *BLEv42* requires *HeartRateSensor* in the graphs only because a rule over three features happens to imply it.

-   `decision_power` is what selecting one feature decides *by itself*. Two selections together can decide more than the sum of what each decides, through a rule over three features ([Configuring](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:configuration)).

-   `level`, `direct_required_by`, `direct_requires`, `betweenness`, `closeness_out`, `closeness_in` and `harmonic` are computed on the skeleton, not on the complete graph: in the complete graph every requirement is one step away, and distances would be meaningless. The skeleton has the limits of [The skeleton](#sec:skeleton).

-   For those measures an atomic set counts as one feature: its members get the values of the set.

:::

## What a feature requires, excludes, or is required by

*Function:* `vp_required_by()`

**The question.** Which features exactly are behind those numbers?

Four functions list them, each as a tibble with one column, `feature`:

|              |                                                        |
|:-------------|:-------------------------------------------------------|
| `vp_required_by(band, f)` | the features that require `f`: its impact set |
| `vp_requirements(band, f)` | the features that `f` requires                |
| `vp_conflicts_of(band, f)` | the features that `f` excludes                |
| `vp_atleastone_of(band, f)` | the features that leaving `f` out forces in   |

```{r required_by}
vp_required_by(band, "Screen")
vp_conflicts_of(band, "BT40")
```

Misspell a name, or give a feature that is not a dot of the graphs, and the function says so:

```{r required_by_error}
try(vp_required_by(band, "Pedometer"))
```

*Pedometer* is core: every band has it, so every feature "requires" it, which says nothing about the choices, and variplex leaves it out of the graphs. (`try()` only lets the example go on after the error; you do not need it.)

**Why it matters.** These lists are the everyday questions of whoever maintains a product line: before changing or removing a feature, who depends on it (its impact set); before selecting it, what it brings along and what it rules out. They are the concrete names behind the counts of the feature table, and exact, so a change can be planned on them.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

The lists are complete for relationships between two features: if a feature is not in `vp_required_by(band, "Screen")`, some valid band has it without a screen. They are silent about rules over three or more features.

:::

## Genuine hubs {#sec:hubs}

*Function:* `vp_genuine_hubs()`

**The question.** Is a feature required by many others in its own right, or only because one popular feature requires it?

Suppose a feature $q$ has a thousand dependants, but almost all of them require $q$ only because they require some feature $p$, and $p$ requires $q$. Then the thousand dependants are really $p$'s dependants: $q$ inherits them. Reviewing $q$ as if it were central would miss the point; $p$ is the feature to watch. Abate et al. call this **strong dominance** [Abate et al. (2009)](#ref-Abate09): $p$ *dominates* $q$ when $p$ requires $q$ and nearly all the other features that require $q$ also require $p$. A hub that no feature dominates is **genuine**: its dependants reach it by many different ways. `vp_genuine_hubs()` works in three steps:

1.  Take the most required features (`top`, 10 by default).

2.  For each of them, $q$, and for each feature $p$ that requires $q$, compute the share of the *other* dependants of $q$ that also require $p$: the column `share_through`.

3.  If some $p$ reaches the threshold `share` (0.9 by default: "nearly all"), $q$ is dominated by the one with the highest share; otherwise $q$ is genuine.

*On the band* the answer is simple:

```{r hubs}
vp_genuine_hubs(band)
```

The screen is genuine: of the 4 features that require it (the atomic set counts as one), none gathers the others. The heart-rate sensor, *BLEv40* and *NFC* are required by a single feature each, which dominates them trivially: *BLEv40* is required only because the movement filter is (the figure). Domination becomes interesting when a hub has many dependants.

![The band: the screen (blue) is a genuine hub, reached directly by three features. *BLEv40* (yellow) is required only by *MovementFilter* (thick arrow): its dependants are the movement filter's, so the movement filter dominates it.](figures/am_hubs.svg){#fig:hubs}

*On Linux 3.13*, the twelve most required options:

```{r hubs_linux}
vp_genuine_hubs(linux, top = 12)
```

`NLATTR`, the library of netlink attributes, is the most required option of the kernel: `r facts$NlattrUsers` options require it (the table counts each atomic set once, hence a smaller number). But `NET` requires it, and of all those options only `r facts$NlattrOthers` reach it without requiring `NET` as well (the figure): `share_through` is 0.999. `NLATTR` is required because networking is; `NET` is the genuine hub. The crypto options show a chain: `CRYPTO` is dominated by `CRYPTO_ALGAPI2`, which is dominated by `CRYPTO_ALGAPI`, which is genuine. Follow `dominated_by` until a genuine hub to find the feature whose dependants they all are.

```{r hubs_facts, include = FALSE}
u <- vp_required_by(linux, "NLATTR")$feature
viaNET <- c("NET", vp_required_by(linux, "NET")$feature)
fact("NlattrUsers", length(u))
fact("NlattrOthers", sum(!u %in% viaNET))
fact("NlattrViaNet", sum(u %in% viaNET) - 1)
fact("NlattrOthersNames", paste0("\\texttt{", setdiff(u, viaNET), "}"))
stopifnot(sum(!u %in% viaNET) == 2)
```

<figure id="fig:hublinux">

<figcaption>Why <code>NLATTR</code> is not a genuine hub of Linux 3.13. Almost all its dependants reach it through <code>NET</code> (thick arrows); only `r facts$NlattrOthers` require it directly without requiring <code>NET</code>. <code>NET</code> dominates it.</figcaption>
</figure>

**Why it matters.** The list of the most required features is long and repetitive: many of its entries are there only because they sit below a big hub. The genuine hubs are the short list of features that really hold the model together, the ones whose review pays off. A fixed threshold such as "required by more than 10 % of the features" [Sanchez et al. (2026)](#ref-P-Variability) cannot make that distinction; domination can.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

Dominance is read from the requires graph, so it inherits its limits. The threshold `share` is a choice: with 0.9, a hub required by 11 features is dominated when 9 of the other 10 also require the same feature. Try other values before drawing conclusions. A feature required by a single feature is always dominated by it, which says little. Only the `top` most required features are examined.

:::

## The warning zone

*Function:* `vp_warning_zone()`

**The question.** Which features are both highly required and highly demanding?

Such features are costly in both directions: many things break when they change, and many things can break them [Sanchez et al. (2026)](#ref-P-Variability). `vp_warning_zone()` returns the rows of the feature table in the top tenth (`top = 0.1`) for both `required_by` and `requires`. The band is too small to have any. Linux 3.13 has `r facts$WzLinux` options in the zone; the most extreme ones first:

```{r warning_zone}
wz <- vp_warning_zone(linux)
nrow(wz)
wz |>
  arrange(desc(required_by * requires)) |>
  select(feature, required_by, requires) |>
  head(5)
```

```{r warning_zone_chart, include = FALSE}
ftl <- vp_feature_table(linux)
hi <- function(x) quantile(x, 0.9, names = FALSE)
p <- ggplot(ftl, aes(required_by + 1, requires + 1)) +
  geom_jitter(
    aes(colour = feature %in% wz$feature),
    width = 0.04,
    height = 0.04,
    alpha = 0.5,
    size = 0.8
  ) +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = hi(ftl$required_by) + 1,
    linetype = "dashed",
    colour = "#8A93A6"
  ) +
  geom_hline(
    yintercept = hi(ftl$requires) + 1,
    linetype = "dashed",
    colour = "#8A93A6"
  ) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  scale_colour_manual(
    values = c(`FALSE` = "#C9CED8", `TRUE` = "#B3261E"),
    guide = "none"
  ) +
  labs(x = "required by + 1 (log scale)", y = "requires + 1 (log scale)")
save_chart(p, "warning_zone", height = 2.8)
fact("WzLinux", nrow(wz))
fact("WzReqBy", hi(ftl$required_by))
fact("WzReq", hi(ftl$requires))
```

![Linux 3.13: one dot per option, on logarithmic axes (plus one, so that 0 can be drawn). The dashed lines are the thresholds of the top tenth: required by at least `r facts$WzReqBy` options, and requiring at least `r facts$WzReq`. The options of the warning zone (red) are beyond both lines: the upper-right corner. The hubs far to the right need little; the options at the top left are needed by nobody.](figures/am_warning_zone.svg){#fig:warningzone}

The cluster at the top of the zone is a family of sound codec drivers (`SND_SOC_*`): each requires more than a hundred options, and each is required by more than sixty. A change to any of them reaches many options, and a change to any of their many requirements can break them: they are the places of the kernel where a careless edit costs most.

**Why it matters.** A feature that both is needed by many and needs many sits at a crossing: an error in it spreads to its dependants, and a change in any of its requirements reaches it. In a product line those are the features where a careless edit costs most, which deserve the first reviews, the most tests and the most careful evolution.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

"Top tenth" is relative to the model: every model has a warning zone, however healthy. Compare its size between models or versions rather than read it alone. The counts behind it are those of the requires graph.

:::

# Layers and hierarchy {#sec:hierarchy}

This section is about the shape of the requires graph: how its features are layered, from those that need nothing to those that need long chains of others. That shape has a name in mathematics, a **partial order** [Davey and Priestley (2002)](#ref-Davey02), and a few classic results about partial orders answer practical questions about variability models. You need none of the theory to use the functions, but the idea is simple, and it explains why the functions work.

## What a partial order is, on the band {#what-a-partial-order-is-on-the-band .unnumbered}

An *order* is a way of saying that one thing comes before another, like the numbers 1 \< 2 \< 3. "$f$ requires $g$" can be read the same way: $g$ comes before $f$, because $g$ must be there for $f$ to be there. Three properties make "requires" an order. Each one can be checked on the band:

```{r order}
band$requires |> filter(from == "MovementFilter")
band$requires |>
  filter(from %in% c("Screen", "OLED"), to %in% c("Screen", "OLED"))
vp_pair(band, "TouchScreen", "BT40") |> select(combination, possible)
```

1.  **It chains (transitivity).** If $f$ requires $g$ and $g$ requires $h$, then $f$ requires $h$. *MovementFilter* requires *BLEv40*, which requires the screen, and indeed the first table has the arrow from *MovementFilter* to *Screen*. variplex's requires graph always holds every such arrow (it is *complete*).

2.  **It never goes round in a circle (antisymmetry)**, once an atomic set counts as one feature. The second table shows that the screen requires the OLED panel *and* the OLED panel requires the screen: they go round in a circle. But two features that require each other are always selected together, so for every choice they are one feature, an atomic set ([Atomic sets](#sec:atomic)). Merge each atomic set into one node, and no circle is left: if $f$ requires $g$, then $g$ does not require $f$.

3.  **Every feature "requires" itself (reflexivity)**: trivially, a product with $f$ has $f$. It is a convention that makes the mathematics tidy; the graphs do not draw these arrows.

Why *partial*? Because in the order of the numbers, of any two numbers one comes before the other: the order is *total*, a single line. In a partial order, two features can be *incomparable*: neither requires the other. The last table shows that *TouchScreen* and *BT40* are such a pair: there are bands with only one of them, so neither requires the other (in fact they cannot go together, but a conflict is not a requirement). Of the 36 pairs of the 9 nodes of the band (its atomic set counted once), only 7 are comparable; the other 29 are not.

![The skeleton of the Mi Band 3, which draws its partial order: a feature is below the features it requires, and one feature comes before another when a chain of arrows leads from the second to the first. *MovementFilter* comes after *BLEv40* and after the screen; *TouchScreen* and *BT40* (yellow), with no chain between them, are incomparable.](figures/am_order.svg){#fig:order}

So a partial order is drawn not as a line but as a set of layers with arrows between them: the skeleton (the figure), which keeps only the arrows between immediate neighbours (in the theory of orders it is called the *Hasse diagram*). Everything in this section is a question about that drawing:

| **Question about the drawing**                                 | **Function, and the result of order theory behind it**                                                                                                                                                                                         |
|:---------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Which features are one node?                                   | `vp_atomic_sets()`: the features that go round in a circle.                                                                                                                                                                                          |
| Which arrows are between immediate neighbours?                 | `vp_skeleton()`: the Hasse diagram (transitive reduction [Aho et al. (1972)](#ref-Aho72)).                                                                                                                                                                               |
| In which layer is each feature, and how many layers are there? | `vp_levels()`, `vp_depth()`: the length of the longest chain (Mirsky's theorem [Mirsky (1971)](#ref-Mirsky71), [A safe order for the questions](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:rounds)).                                                                          |
| How many features are pairwise incomparable, at most?          | `vp_width()`: the largest antichain, equal to the fewest chains that cover all the features (Dilworth's theorem [Dilworth (1950)](#ref-Dilworth50)).                                                                                                                |
| Which features are at the top and at the bottom?               | `vp_foundations()`, `vp_leaves()`: the maximal and minimal elements.                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| What comes with a feature, and what depends on it?             | `vp_upset()`, `vp_downset()`: the up-set and the down-set of the feature.                                                                                                                                                                        |
| And with the conflicts too?                                    | `vp_largest_free_set()` and `vp_longest_decision_chain()`: the counterparts of the width and the depth with the three graphs ([Width and chains](#sec:width) and [Levels, depth and the longest chain](#sec:levels)). |

## Atomic sets {#sec:atomic}

*Function:* `vp_atomic_sets()`

**The question.** Which features are really one single decision?

An **atomic set** is a group of features that require each other, so a product has all of them or none [Benavides et al. (2010)](#ref-Benavides10); [Heß and Molt (2025)](#ref-Hess25):

```{r atomic}
vp_atomic_sets(band)
```

*Screen*, *OLED* and *CapacityButton* are one decision. The first, *Screen*, is the `representative`: it stands for the set in the skeleton and in the other functions of this section. The sets come from the file `__atomic.txt`, or are computed from the requires graph when there is no such file.

**Why it matters.** An atomic set is one decision disguised as several features. For a configurator it means one question instead of several; for testing, one value to vary instead of several; for every analysis of the vignettes, one node. Large atomic sets also hint at features that could be merged, or at constraints written so tightly that options can no longer be chosen apart.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

Exact: every group of features that are always selected together is found, and nothing else. But the atomic sets are about features that are *selected* together; features that are always *opposite* (one exactly when the other is not, like *Screen* and *BT40*) are no atomic set. `vp_pair()` ([Everything about two features](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:pair)) finds them.

:::

## The skeleton {#sec:skeleton}

*Function:* `vp_skeleton()`

**The question.** Which dependencies carry information, and which ones follow from others?

The requires graph is complete (property P1, [Three properties of the graphs](variplex.html#sec:properties)). The **skeleton** keeps only the arrows that do not follow from a chain of others (the *transitive reduction* [Aho et al. (1972)](#ref-Aho72)), with each atomic set as one feature:

```{r skeleton}
vp_skeleton(band)
vp_implied_requires(band)
```

Of the `r facts$BandRequires` arrows, `r facts$BandSkeleton` are in the skeleton (the figure). `vp_implied_requires()` lists the arrows between different features that follow from others, and one feature they pass through: *MovementFilter* requires *Screen* *via* *BLEv40*. The other arrows that are not in the skeleton are inside the atomic set. The skeleton is the same as the file `__requires_skeleton.net` of variplex.

![The requires graph of the band. The grey arrows follow from the blue ones: the arrow from *MovementFilter* to the screen follows from the chain through *BLEv40*, and the arrows inside the dashed box from the atomic set. Only the blue arrows are in the skeleton.](figures/am_requires_redundant.svg){#fig:redundant}


::: {.vp-box .vp-plainwords}
The skeleton is close to what somebody had to write; the complete graph is what everybody has to live with. Compare their sizes and you know how much of the model is implicit ([The whole model in numbers](#sec:model)).

:::

**Why it matters.** The skeleton separates the dependencies that someone had to decide from those that merely follow. Its size against the complete graph measures how much of the model is implicit, which is what makes a model hard to understand by reading it one constraint at a time; and its arrows are the ones to look at when a dependency has to be changed.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

The skeleton is not the list of constraints of the model: an arrow of the skeleton can come from a rule over three features (*BLEv42* requires *HeartRateSensor* is in the skeleton, and no constraint says so), and a constraint that the others imply leaves no arrow of its own. "Follows from a chain of arrows" is the only kind of redundancy it removes: an arrow that follows from a conflict and an at-least-one line stays ([Why two features exclude each other](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:explain)).

:::

## Levels, depth and the longest chain {#sec:levels}

*Function:* `vp_levels()`

**The question.** How is the model layered? How long can a line of reasoning get?

The **level** of a feature is the length of the longest chain of requirements that starts from it: level 0 for a feature that needs nothing, level 1 for one that needs only features of level 0, and so on. The **depth** is the number of features in the longest chain:

```{r levels}
vp_levels(band) |> arrange(level)
vp_depth(band)
vp_longest_chain(band)
```

The band has three layers. *MovementFilter* is alone at the top: it requires *BLEv40*, which requires *Screen*. That is the longest chain, with three features. The figure draws the skeleton in its levels.

![Width and chains](#sec:width)).](figures/am_levels.svg){#fig:levels}

**Why it matters.** The depth is the longest line of reasoning a user, a developer or a configurator has to follow to see why a feature is forced: a model of depth `r facts$LinuxDepth`, like Linux, can force an option through `r facts$LinuxDepthInner` intermediate ones. Deep models are harder to explain, to configure step by step (the safe order of [A safe order for the questions](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:rounds) needs one round per level) and to change, because an edit at the bottom reaches everything above. Depth plays the role of the depth of a feature tree, but it follows the meaning, so it exists for any model, Kconfig included.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

A chain is made of arrows, so a rule over three features that would lengthen a chain ("A needs B or C, and both B and C need D" does make A need D, and the graph has that arrow) counts only through the arrows it implies. Depth also depends on how the model was translated into a formula: two translators of the same Kconfig model can give different depths.

So depth is a *warning flag*, not a verdict: a large depth says "long explanations live here, look closer". It sees only arrows of the requires graph, while a conflict can force a decision just as well. The next paragraphs show how to look closer.

:::

**After the flag: chains with conflicts too.** A requirement "A requires B" is one way a decision forces another. There are three more, all in the graphs: a conflict ("A excludes B": selecting A forces leaving B out), an at-least-one line ("A or B": leaving A out forces selecting B), and the contrapositive of each (leaving B out forces leaving A out). `vp_mig()`, the modal implication graph ([The modal implication graph (MIG)](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:mig)), puts them all in one graph whose nodes are *decisions*: "A" (A selected) and "not A" (A left out). `vp_longest_decision_chain()` finds the longest line of decisions in it, each forcing the next. Decisions that force each other (opposites, atomic sets) are one step, written together with "=":

```{r decision_chain}
vp_longest_decision_chain(band)
vp_longest_decision_chain(history$model[[7]]) # the Mi Band 8
vp_depth(history$model[[7]])
```

On the band the length is the same as the depth, 3, but the chain is another one, and it goes through a conflict: a band without a screen has the classic Bluetooth (*BT40*), which excludes *BLEv40*, and without *BLEv40* there is no movement filter. Depth, which reads only arrows of the requires graph, sees "*MovementFilter* requires *BLEv40* requires *Screen*" and nothing else.

On the Mi Band 8 the chain of decisions is longer than the depth: 7 steps against 6. Selecting Bluetooth 5.2 forces SpO2, which forces the photoplethysmogram, which forces the AMOLED screen and its four companions; so far, requirements. Then a conflict: AMOLED and OLED are alternatives, so OLED and its capacity button are left out, and with them *BLEv40* and the movement filter. Two of the seven steps are invisible to the depth.

| **Model**  |  **Depth**   | **Longest chain of decisions** |        **What the extra steps are**         |
|:-----------|:------------:|:------------------------------:|:-------------------------------------------:|
| Mi Band 3  |      3       |               3                | none, but another chain, through a conflict |
| Mi Band 8  |      6       |               7                |     a conflict between the screen types     |
| Linux 3.13 | `r facts$LinuxDepth` |          `r facts$LinuxDecisionChain`          |                    none                     |

```{r decision_chain_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("LinuxDecisionChain", nrow(vp_longest_decision_chain(linux)))
```

**What is still missing.** The chain of decisions is built from pairs too. A decision that is forced only by two decisions *together* ("A and B together exclude C", a rule over three features) is in no graph, and a chain that needs it is not found. When the chain matters (for example, to bound the number of questions a configurator may have to explain), the last check is the formula itself: take the first decision of the chain, give it to a SAT solver as an assumption, and let the solver propagate (on the DIMACS file that `vp_generate()` keeps with `keep_dimacs = TRUE`); what it forces is the whole consequence, rules over three features included. Measures that count by pairs, depth and chain alike, can only be shorter than that.


## Width and chains {#sec:width}

*Function:* `vp_width()`

**The question.** How many decisions are independent of one another, as far as dependencies go?

Depth measures how *tall* the hierarchy is: the longest line of features each requiring the next. Width measures how *broad* it is. Call two features **related** when one requires the other, directly or through a chain. A set of features is **unrelated** when no two of its features are related: none of them, if selected, forces another one of the set. The **width** is the size of the largest unrelated set.

```{r width}
vp_width(band)
vp_largest_unrelated_set(band)
vp_chains(band)
```

*The largest unrelated set of the band* has five features: *TouchScreen*, *HeartRateSensor*, *BLEv40*, *BT40* and *NFC* (shaded in the figure). Check any pair: the touch screen requires only the screen, *BLEv40* only the screen, the three others nothing, so no feature of the set requires another one of the set.

*Why no sixth feature fits.* `vp_chains()` splits the nine nodes of the skeleton (the atomic set is one) into **chains**: lists in which each feature requires the next. Two features of the same chain are always related, so an unrelated set can take at most *one* feature from each chain. There are five chains, so no unrelated set has more than five features:

| **Chain** | **Its features**             | **In the unrelated set** |
|:----------|:-----------------------------|:------------------------:|
| 1         | *TouchScreen* → screen       |      *TouchScreen*       |
| 2         | *ContactlessPayment* → *NFC* |          *NFC*           |
| 3         | *MovementFilter* → *BLEv40*  |         *BLEv40*         |
| 4         | *BLEv42* → *HeartRateSensor* |    *HeartRateSensor*     |
| 5         | *BT40*                       |          *BT40*          |

The unrelated set of five shows that the width is at least 5; the five chains show that it is at most 5. Dilworth's theorem says that this always happens: the largest unrelated set and the fewest chains that cover all the features have the same size [Dilworth (1950)](#ref-Dilworth50). So the two outputs prove each other, and the width is exactly 5.

*What it tells.* The width is the number of decisions that dependencies leave independent of one another: a configurator can show the five features of the set side by side, and no answer about one of them forces an answer about another (as far as requirements go). A width close to the number of features means a flat model, a long list of independent choices; a small width means a model organised in a few long lines, each a chain of specialisations from a basic feature to its most demanding variants.

**Depth and width together** describe the shape of a model. A model of $n$ features always has $n \le \text{depth} \times \text{width}$, so a large model cannot be both shallow and narrow. Shallow and wide means many independent decisions, each easy to explain; deep and narrow, few decisions with long chains of consequences. The band, with depth 3 and width 5 for 9 nodes, is shallow and wide; so is BusyBox (depth `r facts$BusyDepth`, width `r facts$BusyWidth` for `r facts$BusyFeaturesLast` features); Linux 3.13 is deep and wide (depth `r facts$LinuxDepth`, width `r facts$LinuxWidth`).

```{r width_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("BusyDepth", vp_depth(bb))
fact("BusyWidth", vp_width(bb))
fact("LinuxDepth", vp_depth(linux))
fact("LinuxWidth", vp_width(linux))
fact("LinuxDepthInner", vp_depth(linux) - 2)
```

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

Width looks only at dependencies. Two features that count as unrelated may still exclude each other, or be tied by a rule over three features: "independent" here means "neither requires the other", not "free to combine".

So width is a *warning flag*, an upper bound: the number of truly free decisions is at most the width, and may be much less. The next paragraphs show how to bring conflicts and at-least-one lines in.

:::

**After the flag: free, not just unrelated.** Call two features **free** when the four combinations (both, only the first, only the second, neither) all occur in valid products: then no graph links them, neither requires, excludes nor at-least-one. A set is free when every two of its features are. `vp_largest_free_set()` finds the largest one, with the three graphs:

```{r free_set}
vp_largest_free_set(band)
nrow(vp_largest_free_set(history$model[[7]])) # the Mi Band 8
vp_width(history$model[[7]])
```

The band has four free features against a width of five. (Do not confuse it with the largest *compatible* set of [Conflicts](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:conflicts), `vp_largest_compatible_set()`: compatible features only need to go together, free features must also be able to go apart, in every combination.) *BT40* is gone from the set: it is unrelated to *BLEv40* (neither requires the other), but the two exclude each other, since a band has one kind of Bluetooth. (*NFC* is replaced by *ContactlessPayment*: the two are in the same chain, so either one fits.) On the Mi Band 8 the gap is wide: width 7, but only 3 free features. The Bluetooth versions are unrelated, which makes the width large, and they are alternatives, which makes them anything but free.

| **Model**  |  **Width**   | **Largest free set**  | **Why they differ**                       |
|:-----------|:------------:|:---------------------:|:------------------------------------------|
| Mi Band 3  |      5       |           4           | *BT40* excludes *BLEv40*                  |
| Mi Band 8  |      7       |           3           | the Bluetooth versions are alternatives   |
| Linux 3.13 | `r facts$LinuxWidth` | at least `r facts$LinuxFree` | conflicts (search not exact at this size) |

```{r free_set_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("LinuxFree", nrow(suppressMessages(vp_largest_free_set(linux))))
```

**The third level: free in pairs is not free together.** Every two features of a free set take their four combinations; that does not mean the whole set takes its $2^k$ combinations. A rule over three features can forbid one. With the 24 products of the band (the table `products` of [Measuring each feature](#sec:features)) the check is a count:

```{r free_together}
free <- vp_largest_free_set(band)$feature
products |> count(across(all_of(free)))
```

Fourteen of the sixteen combinations occur. The two missing ones are a touch screen with neither the heart-rate sensor nor *BLEv40*. A band with a screen cannot have the classic Bluetooth, so it has BLE, and the model says "BLE needs the heart-rate sensor or the movement filter"; the movement filter needs *BLEv40*. So a touch screen needs the sensor or *BLEv40*: a rule over three features that no pair shows. So, of the four free features, a configurator can offer any two side by side, but not all four as independent switches.

When the products cannot be listed, the same question goes to the formula: one call of a SAT solver per combination ($2^k$ calls, with each combination given as assumptions), or one call of a model counter such as GANAK per combination, which also says how many products have it. For $k$ up to about 15 this is quick. It is the only exact answer: the graphs can flag the problem, and the formula settles it.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

`vp_largest_free_set()` solves a hard problem (a largest independent set). Up to 60 variable features (`exact_limit`) the search is exact; above it, a greedy search gives a free set that cannot be extended, and the largest one may be larger: on Linux the answer is a lower bound. It is also about pairs, and the third level above is what it cannot see.

:::

::: {.vp-box .vp-inside}
**How it works inside**

The width comes from a maximum matching in a graph with two copies of each feature, one as "requires" and one as "is required" (`igraph::max_bipartite_match()`): each matched pair is a link of a chain, so the chains are the features minus the matched pairs. König's theorem turns the same matching into a largest set of unrelated features. The depth comes from one pass over the features in topological order. Both are fast on Linux.

:::

## Foundations and leaves

*Function:* `vp_foundations()`

**The question.** Which features are the base of everything, and which ones could be removed without breaking anything?

```{r foundations}
vp_foundations(band)
vp_leaves(band)
```

**Foundations** need nothing: every chain of requirements ends in one of them. **Leaves** are needed by nothing: removing one breaks no other feature. Some features are both, like *BT40*: in the skeleton they stand alone.

**Why it matters.** Foundations are where the product line starts: the features every chain of requirements ends in, which must be designed, tested and kept stable first. Leaves are where it ends: features nothing depends on, which can be removed or changed without breaking another feature, and where new features are usually attached. The two lists are the two ends of a refactoring or a clean-up.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

"Removing a leaf breaks no other feature" is true of the requires graph. A rule over three features ("A needs B or C") can still lean on a leaf: remove C, and A needs B.

:::

## What a selection drags in, and what depends on it

*Function:* `vp_upset()`

**The question.** If I select these features, what else comes with them? If I change this feature, what is affected?

The **up-set** of some features is themselves and everything they require; the **down-set** is themselves and everything that requires one of them. `vp_upset()` says which given feature brings each one; `vp_downset()` which given feature each one depends on:

```{r upset}
vp_upset(band, c("MovementFilter", "NFC"))
vp_downset(band, "BLEv40")
```

The figure shows the up-set of the two features on the skeleton. Two more functions find where two features meet: `vp_common_requirements(band, a, b)` (what both need: shared infrastructure) and `vp_common_dependants(band, a, b)` (what needs both: where two parts of the model are tied together):

```{r common}
vp_common_requirements(band, "MovementFilter", "BLEv42")
vp_common_dependants(band, "Screen", "HeartRateSensor")
```

The movement filter and Bluetooth 4.2 share the screen and its atomic set (the figure); nothing requires both the screen and the heart-rate sensor except *BLEv42*.

![The up-set of *MovementFilter* and *NFC* (yellow): the features they bring along, in green, are those reached by following the arrows upwards.](figures/am_upset.svg){#fig:upset}

![The common requirements of *MovementFilter* and *BLEv42* (yellow): the screen and its atomic set (green), which both reach by following the arrows.](figures/am_common.svg){#fig:common}

**Why it matters.** The up-set is what a customer really gets when choosing some features, and what a product will contain whether or not anyone asked for it; the down-set is everything a change to a feature can affect. Both are what impact analysis and configuration explanations are built on.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

The up-set is what selecting the features forces *through requirements*. It is not everything a selection settles: it leaves out what the features exclude (see `vp_consequences()`, [What some decisions force](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:consequences)), and, through rules over three or more features, two selections together can force a feature that neither forces alone.

:::

## A feature tree rebuilt from the graphs {#sec:tree}

*Function:* `vp_feature_tree()`

**The question.** What feature tree do the dependencies suggest? Useful for Kconfig, which has no tree, and to compare with the tree of a UVL model.

Each feature gets as parent one of the features it requires directly in the skeleton. When there are several candidates, the least required one is chosen (the simplest of the rules studied in the work on feature-model synthesis [Czarnecki and Wąsowski (2007)](#ref-Czarnecki07); [She et al. (2011)](#ref-She11); [Andersen et al. (2012)](#ref-Andersen12)), and the others become cross-tree requirements:

```{r tree}
vp_feature_tree(band)
```


![The tree that the skeleton of the band suggests: black lines join a feature (below) to the parent chosen for it (above). The dashed blue arrow is a requirement that the tree cannot hold: *BLEv42* needs the screen too.](figures/am_feature_tree.svg){#fig:tree}

Compare with the figure. *BLEv42* hangs from *HeartRateSensor* instead of from *BLE*, which is abstract and so not in the graphs, and the screen becomes a cross-tree requirement. *Screen*, *HeartRateSensor*, *BT40* and *NFC* need nothing, so they have no parent (`NA`): they are the tops of the tree, where a feature model would hang them from its root.

**Models without a tree.** `vp_feature_tree()` reads only the graphs, never the file the model came from, so it works on every model variplex accepts: UVL, a DIMACS formula of any origin, or Kconfig (through its DIMACS translation). The last two have no feature tree at all, and that is where the function is most useful. BusyBox is a Kconfig model; the package has only its graphs, from the published dataset ([The examples](reading-and-drawing.html#sec:examples)), not its Kconfig files nor its formula, and that is enough:

```{r tree_busybox}
tree_bb <- vp_feature_tree(bb)
c(
  features = nrow(tree_bb),
  tops = sum(is.na(tree_bb$parent)),
  one_candidate = sum(tree_bb$candidates == 1),
  with_cross_tree = sum(tree_bb$cross_tree != "")
)
tree_bb |> count(parent, sort = TRUE) |> filter(!is.na(parent)) |> head(3)
tree_bb |> filter(parent == "CONFIG_FIND") |> select(feature, parent) |> head(4)
```

The rebuilt tree gives BusyBox a shape from its graphs alone: each applet (`find`, `ash`, `hush`) becomes a parent, with its options as children. `find` alone has twenty: `-prune`, `-xdev`, `-mtime` and the rest. For the features with one candidate (`r facts$BusyOneCandidate` of `r facts$BusyTreeFeatures`) there is only one possible parent, so for them the tree is not a guess. The `r facts$BusyTops` tops, features that need nothing, would hang from the root of a feature model, and `r facts$BusyCrossTree` features keep a cross-tree requirement.

```{r tree_busybox_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("BusyTreeFeatures", nrow(tree_bb))
fact("BusyOneCandidate", sum(tree_bb$candidates == 1))
fact("BusyTops", sum(is.na(tree_bb$parent)))
fact("BusyCrossTree", sum(tree_bb$cross_tree != ""))
```

For a model without a tree, this is a first feature diagram, to read the model or to start a UVL version of it. Whether the tree agrees with the menus of the Kconfig files is a separate question, which needs those files.

**Using the rebuilt tree to refactor a model.** A **refactoring** changes how a model is written and keeps its products: the same valid configurations, before and after [Thüm et al. (2009)](#ref-Thuem09). A common reason to refactor is to turn cross-tree constraints into tree edges. A constraint "A requires B" says, in the margin, what the tree could say by hanging A under B. Fewer constraints make a model easier to read, and the tree carries the meaning where the reader looks first.

The rebuilt tree is a list of such proposals. Every parent it chooses is a true requirement of the child, so hanging the child there never loses a product. The Mi Band 8 has 22 constraint lines. Its rebuilt tree and its graphs suggest three of them can go:

```{r refactor_proposals}
tree8_rebuilt <- vp_feature_tree(history$model[[7]])
tree8_rebuilt |> filter(feature %in% c("SpO2", "ContactlessPayment"))
vp_atomic_sets(history$model[[7]]) |>
  filter(feature %in% c("NFC", "ContactlessPayment"))
```

-   *SpO2* hangs from *Sensors* in the model and needs the photoplethysmogram through the constraint `SpO2 => Photoplethysmogram`. The rebuilt tree puts it under *Photoplethysmogram*. Move it there as an optional child, and the constraint goes.

-   *NFC* and *ContactlessPayment* are an atomic set: two constraints say that each requires the other. Make *NFC* a mandatory child of *ContactlessPayment*, and both constraints go.

The changes, from the realized model to the refactored one:

| **Where**   | **Before**                       | **After**                               |
|:---------------------|:---------------------|:---------------------|
| *NFC*       | optional child of *Connectivity* | mandatory child of *ContactlessPayment* |
| *SpO2*      | optional child of *Sensors*      | optional child of *Photoplethysmogram*  |
| Constraints | `NFC => ContactlessPayment`                     | deleted                                 |
|             | `ContactlessPayment => NFC`                     | deleted                                 |
|             | `SpO2 => Photoplethysmogram`                     | deleted                                 |

**Check that it is a refactoring.** Run variplex on both models (the new one is `vp_example("miband8_refactored.uvl")`), with `representativeness = TRUE` so that it counts the products (this needs the model counter GANAK), and compare. Identical graphs and the same number of products are two necessary conditions:

```{r refactor_check}
before <- vp_generate(
  vp_example("miband8_realized.uvl"),
  or_graph = TRUE,
  representativeness = TRUE
)
after <- vp_generate(
  vp_example("miband8_refactored.uvl"),
  or_graph = TRUE,
  representativeness = TRUE
)
vp_compare(before, after) |>
  select(graph, added, removed, gained, lost, jaccard)
c(
  before = vp_representativeness(before)$vm_configurations,
  after = vp_representativeness(after)$vm_configurations
)
```

No feature added or removed, no link gained or lost, 21 products before and after. Neither condition alone proves the refactoring: two formulas can have the same graphs and differ in a rule over three features, and two formulas can have the same number of products and different products. The proof is that each formula implies the other, which a SAT solver checks in two calls [Thüm et al. (2009)](#ref-Thuem09). For this small model the 21 products of each version were listed and compared for the vignettes: they are the same.

**Where not to follow the tree.** The rebuilt tree also hangs the photoplethysmogram under the AMOLED screen, and the gyroscope and the proximity sensor are in the atomic set of the AMOLED screen, so the graphs would make them mandatory children of the screen. Both moves keep the products, and both remove constraints. Both are bad trees: a sensor is not a part of a screen. The constraints say a business fact (the premium screen comes with premium sensors), not a part-of relation, and a tree that hides that fact behind a part-of edge misleads the reader. The graphs propose by meaning; the designer decides by the domain.

**Why it matters.** Feature models are read through their trees, and models from Kconfig or plain formulas have none. A tree rebuilt from the meaning gives such models a first diagram, and for UVL models it is a second opinion on the declared tree: where the two disagree, either the tree hides a layer or a refactoring is possible.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

-   A tree is a proposal, not a discovery. The graphs give each feature its candidate parents; the choice among them is a rule of thumb. The rebuilt tree finds *where* a feature can hang without losing products; it does not say where it *should*.

-   Groups of the model ("exactly one of these children") are rules over many features, so the rebuilt tree has no groups: the alternatives appear as conflicts between siblings. Abstract features, left out of the graphs, cannot become parents. Both have to be added by hand.

-   A refactoring must be proved on the formula, as above: the graphs are evidence, not proof.

:::

## Operations that need the feature tree {#sec:withtree .unnumbered}

The next three operations need the tree of the model, read with `vp_read_uvl_tree()`. Do not confuse it with `vp_feature_tree()`: they have similar names and answer opposite questions.

|                  | `vp_read_uvl_tree(file)`                             | `vp_feature_tree(model)`                                  |
|:--------------|:-------------------------|:-------------------------|
| Reads            | the UVL file, as written by its designer | the graphs that variplex computed            |
| Answers          | "what tree did the designer draw?"       | "what tree does the meaning suggest?"         |
| Features         | all of them: abstract, core and dead too | only those in the graphs (variable, concrete) |
| Kinds of child   | mandatory, optional, alternative, or     | none: every parent is a requirement;          |
|                  | (the `relation` column)                | the rest goes to `cross_tree`                 |
| Works on Kconfig | no (Kconfig has no UVL tree)             | yes                                           |
| Is it the truth? | it is the model, whatever it means       | a proposal: a rule of thumb picks the parent  |

`vp_read_uvl_tree()` does not look at the graphs at all, and `vp_feature_tree()` does not look at the UVL file. The operations of this subsection put the two side by side: the drawing of the model against what its constraints mean.

## False-optional features

*Function:* `vp_false_optional()`

**The question.** Which features are declared optional and are not?

A classic analysis of feature models [Benavides et al. (2010)](#ref-Benavides10). Two definitions are in use: an optional feature is false-optional when it is in every product, or, the definition that Nieke et al. consider more sensible [Nieke et al. (2018)](#ref-Nieke18), when it is selected whenever its parent is. The graphs decide both: the first is the list of core features, the second an arrow from the parent to the child. uClibc, one of the examples of variplex, has both kinds:

```{r false_optional}
uclibc <- vp_read("uclibc/uClibc")
tree_u <- vp_read_uvl_tree("data/uClibc.uvl")
vp_false_optional(uclibc, tree_u)
```

The rows with `in_every_product` `TRUE` are core: every product has them, although the tree says they are optional. The others are selected in every product that has their parent: a C library with crypt support always has the crypt implementation, and one with floating point always has the option of the FPU.

**Why it matters.** A false-optional feature is a defect of a feature model in the classic catalogue: the tree promises a choice that the constraints take away. Users are offered an option that is not one, documentation describes variability that does not exist, and tests may be planned for configurations that cannot be built.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

Exact for both definitions, when the parent is in the graphs. When the parent is abstract (left out of the graphs), the second definition cannot be checked and `whenever_parent` is `NA`; run `vp_generate()` with `abstract = "keep"` to keep the abstract features.

:::

## Does the tree hide layers?

*Function:* `vp_tree_agreement()`

**The question.** Is each feature's declared parent its immediate requirement, or do other features stand in between?

In a feature tree a child requires its parent. When the child reaches its parent only through other features (it requires something that requires the parent), the tree hides a layer of the hierarchy: a "hierarchy violation" in the sense of Berger and Guo [Berger and Guo (2014)](#ref-Berger14), measured by meaning. The Mi Band 8 has one:

```{r tree_agreement}
band8 <- history$model[[7]]
tree8 <- vp_read_uvl_tree("data/miband8_realized.uvl")
vp_tree_agreement(band8, tree8)
```

The tree puts the photoplethysmogram sensor under the heart-rate sensor (the figure), but the constraints make it need the AMOLED screen, which needs the touch screen, which needs the heart-rate sensor. The share of `FALSE` rows measures how far the drawing of a model is from its meaning.

**Why it matters.** People read a feature model by its tree, so a hidden layer is a hidden cost, and each `FALSE` row is worth a look for one of these reasons:

-   *The reader is misled.* The tree shows the photoplethysmogram as a small option of the heart-rate sensor. In fact, selecting it brings in the AMOLED screen, the touch screen, a Bluetooth 5 version, the gyroscope and the proximity sensor. A product manager who prices the option by the tree prices it wrong.

-   *The configurator asks in the wrong order.* A configurator that follows the tree asks about the heart-rate sensor, then about the photoplethysmogram, and only later about the screen. A user who says yes to the photoplethysmogram then finds the screen already decided, or, if the screen was asked first and refused, finds the option disabled for no visible reason. The safe order of [A safe order for the questions](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:rounds) asks about the screen first.

-   *It may be a bug.* Either the tree is out of date (the feature has moved in the product, and the drawing has not followed: a refactoring candidate, see `vp_feature_tree()`), or a constraint is stronger than intended. Here the constraint is `Photoplethysmogram => BLEv50 | BLEv52 | BLEv51`, about Bluetooth. It drags in the screen only because another constraint ties those Bluetooth versions to the AMOLED screen. That is probably a fact of the product line, but it is worth a question to its designer.

-   *It tracks drift.* Across versions, a growing share of `FALSE` rows means the drawing and the meaning are drifting apart, the erosion that Berger and Guo measured in real models [Berger and Guo (2014)](#ref-Berger14).

![Mi Band 8. The tree (dashed line) makes the photoplethysmogram sensor a child of the heart-rate sensor; the skeleton (blue arrows) shows two layers in between.](figures/am_tree_agreement.svg){#fig:treeagreement}

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

Only pairs whose child and parent are both in the graphs are compared: abstract parents, and core ones, are left out. The comparison is with the skeleton, so it inherits its limit: a rule over three features does not appear as a layer.

:::

## Candidate redundant constraints

*Function:* `vp_redundant_candidates()`

**The question.** Which constraints of the model say nothing new, and could be deleted without changing its products?

**What a redundant constraint is.** Suppose a model says "the movement filter requires BLE 4.0" and "BLE 4.0 requires the screen". If someone also writes "the movement filter requires the screen", that third constraint is **redundant**: it follows from the other two, so deleting it leaves exactly the same products. Redundant constraints are common in models that many people edit over the years. They do no harm to the products, but they make the model longer to read, and they mislead: whoever later changes "BLE 4.0 requires the screen" may not notice that a copy of its consequence is written elsewhere, and may wonder why the movement filter still needs the screen.

**The rule.** A written requirement "A requires B" is redundant, in this sense, when A reaches B through a chain of *other* requirements, A $\to$ X $\to \cdots \to$ B. The skeleton ([The skeleton](#sec:skeleton)) is exactly the set of arrows that no such chain explains. So the test is simple: *a written requirement that is not an arrow of the skeleton is redundant*; one that is an arrow of the skeleton carries information of its own.

**What to give it.** The graphs do not know which constraints were written; you tell the function, as a tibble with the columns `from` and `to`, one row per written requirement "`from` requires `to`". A first example on the band: five requirements someone might have written, among them the third constraint of the story above, and one that is false:

```{r redundant_band}
written_band <- tribble(
  ~from                , ~to      ,
  "MovementFilter"     , "BLEv40" ,
  "MovementFilter"     , "Screen" ,
  "ContactlessPayment" , "NFC"    ,
  "OLED"               , "Screen" ,
  "TouchScreen"        , "BLEv40"
)
vp_redundant_candidates(band, written_band)
```

Read it with the skeleton of the band in the figure:

| **Written requirement**      | **What the columns say**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |
|:-----------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| *MovementFilter* → *BLEv40*  | `in_graphs` `TRUE`: the model implies it. `redundant` `FALSE`: it is an arrow of the skeleton, no other chain gives it. Keep it.                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| *MovementFilter* → *Screen*  | `redundant` `TRUE`, `via` *BLEv40*: the chain *MovementFilter* → *BLEv40* → screen already gives it. A candidate for deletion.                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| *ContactlessPayment* → *NFC* | Not redundant: nothing else makes contactless payment need NFC.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
| *OLED* → *Screen*            | `via` "same atomic set": the two are always selected together, so each requires the other and neither arrow can be removed without changing what the set means. Not a candidate.                                                                                                                                                                 |
| *TouchScreen* → *BLEv40*     | `in_graphs` `FALSE`: the model does *not* imply it (there are bands with a touch screen and BLE 4.2). A written requirement that the graphs do not hold means that the list you gave does not match the model: a typo, a constraint from another version, or a constraint that something else overrides. Worth checking, for the opposite reason. |

**On a real model.** In a UVL model, the written requirements are of two kinds: the edges of the tree (a child requires its parent) and the cross-tree constraints of the form `A => B`. A constraint such as `MovementFilter => !HeartRateSensor & BLEv40` contains one requirement, *MovementFilter* → *BLEv40*, and a conflict, which is not a requirement. The Mi Band 8, with the tree edges between features that can vary (core and abstract features are not in the graphs) and three of its constraints:

```{r redundant}
vars <- vp_features(band8) |> filter(status == "variable") |> pull(feature)
# the tree: a child requires its parent
written <- tree8 |>
  filter(feature %in% vars, parent %in% vars) |>
  select(from = feature, to = parent) |>
  # and three constraints
  bind_rows(tibble(
    from = c("MovementFilter", "SpO2", "BLEv52"),
    to = c("BLEv40", "Photoplethysmogram", "SpO2")
  ))
vp_redundant_candidates(band8, written)
```

One candidate: the tree edge from the photoplethysmogram sensor to the heart-rate sensor, redundant `via` the AMOLED screen. It is the hidden layer of the previous operation seen from the other side: the constraints already make the sensor need the AMOLED screen, which needs the touch screen, which needs the heart-rate sensor, so the tree edge adds nothing. A tree edge cannot simply be deleted (every feature hangs from somewhere), but it can be moved: hang the photoplethysmogram sensor under the AMOLED screen, as the rebuilt tree suggests ([A feature tree rebuilt from the graphs](#sec:tree)), and the hierarchy says what the constraints mean. The other written requirements carry information of their own, or join features of one atomic set.

**Why it matters.** A redundant constraint is one more line to read and one more place to update: whoever changes one of the constraints it repeats must find it too, or the model ends up saying two different things. Finding the redundant ones lets a model say each thing once.

**Before deleting.** Treat each candidate as a proposal and confirm it the way a refactoring is confirmed: delete the constraint, run variplex on both versions and check that the graphs and the number of products are the same, or, for a proof, that each formula implies the other.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

-   "Redundant" here means "implied by a chain of requirements". A requirement can also follow from a conflict and an at-least-one line (`vp_requires_from_conflicts()`, [Conflicts](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:conflicts)), or from rules over three features; those stay arrows of the skeleton and are not flagged. So a `FALSE` in `redundant` does not prove that the constraint is needed.

-   It only examines requirements between two features: a constraint over three or more features, or a conflict, cannot be given to it.

-   The same requirement written twice (two constraints that say the same) is one arrow of the graphs: give the list without duplicates, and look for duplicates in the text of the model.

-   Removing one redundant constraint can make another one necessary: if `A => B`, `B => C` and `A => C` are written and `B => C` is later deleted, `A => C` stops being redundant. Delete one at a time, and check after each.

:::

# The whole model in numbers {#sec:model}

## One row per model

*Function:* `vp_summary()`

**The question.** How does this model compare with others? Has it become more entangled from one version to the next?

`vp_summary()` sums a model up in one row. Give it several models, with names, and it stacks their rows. The band, the last month of BusyBox and Linux 3.13, side by side (`t()` turns the table so that each model is a column):

```{r summary}
s <- vp_summary(band = band, busybox = bb, linux = linux)
s |> select(-form) |> tibble::column_to_rownames("model") |> t() |> round(3)
```

The formulas use the notation of [Measuring each feature](#sec:features), and three more symbols: $V'$, $\Rightarrow'$ and $\#'$ are the features and links once each atomic set counts as one node, $n' = |V'|$; $P$ is the set of primitive conflicts; and $r$ the number of nodes of $V'$ that require nothing.

| **Column**                               | **Formula**                                                                                  | **Meaning**                                                                                                                                                                             |
|:-----------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `features`, `core`, `dead` | $n$, and the sizes of the two lists                                                          | The features that can vary (the dots), and those in every product or in none.                                                                                                           |
| `atomic_sets`                             |                                                                                              | Atomic sets of two or more features.                                                                                                                                                    |
| `requires`, `excludes`, `atleastone` | $|{\Rightarrow}|$, $|{\#}|$, $|{\vee}|$                                                      | The links of the three graphs.                                                                                                                                                          |
| `skeleton`, `primitive_conflicts`               | $|S|$, $|P|$                                                                                 | The links of the minimal form ([The skeleton](#sec:skeleton) and [Primitive conflicts](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:primitive)). |
| `implicit_requires`                             | $1 - \dfrac{|S|}{|{\Rightarrow'}|}$                                                          | The share of the dependencies that follow from others.                                                                                                                                  |
| `implicit_excludes`                             | $1 - \dfrac{|P|}{|{\#'}|}$                                                                   | The share of the conflicts that are inherited from a primitive one.                                                                                                                     |
| `density_requires`                             | $\dfrac{|{\Rightarrow}|}{n(n-1)}$                                                            | Arrows over the possible ones: the chance that a change to a random feature affects another random feature, the *propagation cost* of MacCormack et al. [MacCormack et al. (2006)](#ref-MacCormack06).                |
| `density_excludes`                             | $\dfrac{|{\#}|}{n(n-1)/2}$                                                                   | Conflicts over the possible ones: the share of pairs that no product has together.                                                                                                      |
| `depth`                             | $1 + \max_{f} \text{level}(f)$                                                               | The number of features of the longest chain ([Levels, depth and the longest chain](#sec:levels)).                                                                  |
| `width`                             | $\max |W|$, over the sets $W \subseteq V'$ with no $f \Rightarrow g$ inside                  | The largest set of features none of which requires another ([Width and chains](#sec:width)).                                                     |
| `cross_tree_share`                             | $1 - \dfrac{n' - r}{|S|}$                                                                    | How far the skeleton is from a tree, which would have one arrow per node that requires something.                                                                                       |
| `hierarchy_share`                             | $\dfrac{|{\Rightarrow'}|}{n'(n'-1)/2}$                                                       | The share of pairs related by a requirement: 0 for a flat list of choices, 1 for a single chain.                                                                                        |
| `gini_required_by`, `gini_excludes`               | $\dfrac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} (2i - n - 1)\, x_{(i)}}{n \sum_{i} x_i}$, with $x_{(1)} \le \dots \le 
                                            x_{(n)}$ the values sorted                                                                    | How unevenly the dependants (`required_by`) and the conflicts (`excludes`) are spread: 0 if every feature has as many, near 1 if one feature has them all (`vp_gini()`).              |
| `mean_decision_power`                             | $\dfrac{1}{n}\sum_{f \in V} \text{decision\_power}(f)$                                       | The average share of the model that selecting one feature decides.                                                                                                                      |
| `isolated`                             | $|\{f \in V : f \text{ is isolated}\}|$                                                      | Features in no graph.                                                                                                                                                                   |
| `coupling`                             | $1 - \dfrac{\text{isolated}}{n}$                                                             | The share of features with at least one strong relationship: the counterpart, by meaning, of the cross-tree constraint ratio of the literature.                                         |
| `skeleton_per_feature`                             | $\dfrac{|S|}{n'}$                                                                            | Links of the skeleton per node: density by meaning.                                                                                                                                     |
| `largest_atomic_set`                             | $\max |A|$, over the atomic sets $A$                                                         | The largest group of mutually dependent features: what Baldwin et al. call the "core" of an architecture [Baldwin et al. (2014)](#ref-Baldwin14).                                                                  |
| `largest_alternative_group`                             | $\max |K|$, over the sets $K \subseteq V$ with $f \mathbin{\#} g$ for all $f \ne g$ in $K$   | The most features that all exclude one another ([Groups of alternatives](conflicts-configuring.html#sec:alternatives)).                                                   |

**What each number means for a product line.**

-   `features`, `core`, `dead`: the real choices of the line, and how many declared options are no choice at all (core options are in every product, dead ones in none: both are worth a look, as false-optional features or left-overs).

-   `requires`, `excludes`, `atleastone`, `skeleton`, `primitive_conflicts`: how many relationships users and maintainers live with, and how many were decided.

-   `implicit_requires`, `implicit_excludes`: how much of the model nobody wrote. High values mean that reading the constraints one by one shows little of what the model forbids or forces: explanations, impact analysis and tool support are needed.

-   `density_requires` (propagation cost) and `density_excludes`: how likely a change to one feature reaches another, and how much of the space of pairs is forbidden. High values mean costly changes and tightly constrained products.

-   `depth`, `width`, `hierarchy_share`, `cross_tree_share`: the shape of the line. Deep means long explanations and many configuration rounds; wide, many independent choices; a high cross-tree share, a hierarchy far from a tree, which a feature diagram will draw poorly.

-   `gini_required_by`, `gini_excludes`: whether a few features carry the dependencies or the conflicts, that is, whether the line rests on a few critical features.

-   `mean_decision_power`: how fast a configuration session converges. Low values mean that users take most decisions one by one.

-   `isolated`, `coupling`: how many options stand alone (free to combine, cheap to test) and how entangled the rest is.

-   `skeleton_per_feature`: how many decided dependencies each feature carries, a size-free measure of how much was written.

-   `largest_atomic_set`, `largest_alternative_group`: the largest block of features that must go together, and the largest choice of "one of these"; the second is also a floor on the products any test campaign must build.

Read across. The three models differ most in how implicit they are and in how much a selection decides:

-   In Linux, `r facts$LinuxImplicitReq` % of the dependencies and `r facts$LinuxImplicitExc` % of the conflicts follow from others: a few `r facts$LinuxPrimitive` primitive conflicts produce `r facts$LinuxExcludes`. Nobody can see them by reading one constraint at a time.

-   In BusyBox only `r facts$BusyImplicitReq` % of the dependencies are implicit: its dependencies are short and mostly written.

-   Selecting a feature of the band decides `r facts$BandPower` % of the others on average; in BusyBox `r facts$BusyPower` %, in Linux `r facts$LinuxPower` %. In a large model each decision settles little, and the user takes most of them one by one.

```{r summary_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("LinuxImplicitReq", pct(s$implicit_requires[3]))
fact("LinuxImplicitExc", pct(s$implicit_excludes[3], 1))
fact("LinuxPrimitive", s$primitive_conflicts[3])
fact("BusyImplicitReq", pct(s$implicit_requires[2]))
fact("BandPower", pct(s$mean_decision_power[1]))
fact("BusyPower", pct(s$mean_decision_power[2], 2))
fact("LinuxPower", pct(s$mean_decision_power[3], 2))
t0 <- Sys.time()
invisible(vp_summary(linux))
fact("LinuxSummarySeconds", round(as.numeric(Sys.time() - t0, units = "secs")))
```

::: {.vp-box .vp-goodtoknow}
`vp_summary(linux)` takes about `r facts$LinuxSummarySeconds` seconds on a laptop. Every function of the vignettes works at that scale, except those that say otherwise (they try every combination, or build a graph of pairs of features).

:::

**Why it matters.** One row per model is what makes models and versions comparable: is this product line more entangled than that one, has it become more implicit since last year, does a translator produce models of a different shape?

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

-   Every number describes the graphs, not the model: a rule over three or more features changes none of them unless it implies a relationship between two ([Limits and theory](limits-theory.html) shows a rule that changes nothing).

-   The numbers depend on what counts as a feature. Graphs that still hold helper variables of a translation (the published dataset of version 1 of Strong4VM, for Kconfig) give very different values; filter them first ([Graphs made by version 1 of Strong4VM](reading-and-drawing.html#sec:version1)).

-   The implicit shares say how many relationships follow from others *in the graphs*; the share of written constraints that are redundant would need the model.

-   `largest_alternative_group` is computed exactly; on graphs with hundreds of thousands of conflicts this can take long.

:::

## Are the files consistent?

*Function:* `vp_check()`

**The question.** Were the files damaged, or filtered by hand in one graph and not in the other?

The graphs of any model have properties that `vp_check()` verifies: one row per property, with the number of cases that break it.

```{r check}
vp_check(band)
```

Among them are properties P1 and P2 of [Three properties of the graphs](variplex.html#sec:properties). A `FALSE` means that the files are incomplete, and every other measure of the vignettes assumes that they are not. The same checks run at the end of every run of `vp_generate()`; this function is for graphs you have read from elsewhere, filtered or edited in R.

**Why it matters.** Every analysis of the vignettes trusts the files. Graphs filtered by hand, merged from several runs, or damaged in transfer can break the properties that the analyses rely on, and give wrong answers without any error. Checking them first is cheap insurance.

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

The checks test the files against each other, not against the model. Files that pass them can still come from a model other than the one you think, or miss a relationship that the model implies; only a new run of variplex settles that.

:::

## Degree distributions and concentration

*Function:* `vp_degree_distribution()`

**The question.** Do a few features carry most of the dependencies, or are the dependencies spread over many?

Two tools answer it. The **degree distribution** is the full picture: how many features have each number of dependants. The **Gini coefficient** sums that picture up in one number, which can be compared between models and followed through versions.

### The degree distribution

The **degree** of a feature, here, is how many features require it: its `required_by`. `vp_degree_distribution()` counts how many features have each degree. On the band it is short enough to read whole:

```{r degrees_band}
vp_degree_distribution(band, "requires")
vp_degree_distribution(band, "skeleton")
```

Each row says: `features` features are required by exactly `degree` others. In the complete requires graph, 5 features are required by nobody (*TouchScreen*, *ContactlessPayment*, *MovementFilter*, *BLEv42*, *BT40*), 3 by one feature each (*HeartRateSensor*, *BLEv40*, *NFC*), and 3 by six each (the screen and the two members of its atomic set). In the skeleton the atomic set is one node, required directly by three features, hence the row "3, 1".

**How to read the figure.** The chart draws such a table for BusyBox, one dot per row: the dot at $x = k$, $y = m$ means "$m$ features are required by exactly $k$ others". Both axes are *logarithmic*: each step along an axis multiplies by ten (1, 10, 100), so that the many features with few dependants and the few features with many dependants fit in one picture. The features required by nobody (degree 0, 419 of them) cannot be drawn on a logarithmic axis and are left out. Read it from left to right:

-   *Top left*: 92 features are required by exactly one feature, 44 by two, 14 by three. Most features that are required at all are required by very few.

-   *Bottom right*: single features (*y* = 1) with many dependants. The rightmost blue dot is `CONFIG_FEATURE_SYSLOG`, required by 45 features.

-   *The two colours*: the blue dots count all the dependants (the complete graph), the orange dots only the direct ones (the skeleton). `CONFIG_FEATURE_SYSLOG` has 45 dependants but only 15 direct ones: the other 30 reach it through chains. That is why the blue tail reaches further right: a feature high in the hierarchy inherits every dependant below it.

A distribution that falls as a straight line on these axes is the signature of a "power law" ([Is it a power law?](patterns.html#sec:powerlaw) tests whether it really is one). The dots of BusyBox fall steeply, then scatter at the right, where there is one feature per degree.

The same table for the last month of BusyBox, whose rows are the dots of the chart:

```{r degrees}
vp_degree_distribution(bb, "requires") |> head(5)
```

```{r degree_facts, include = FALSE}
fact("BusyGini", round(vp_gini(vp_feature_table(bb)$required_by), 2))
```

```{r degree_charts, include = FALSE}
dd <- bind_rows(
  vp_degree_distribution(bb, "requires") |>
    mutate(graph = "complete requires graph"),
  vp_degree_distribution(bb, "skeleton") |> mutate(graph = "skeleton")
) |>
  filter(degree > 0)
p <- ggplot(dd, aes(degree, features, colour = graph)) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#0B3D91", "#C25E00")) +
  labs(
    x = "required by (features), log scale",
    y = "features, log scale",
    colour = NULL
  )
save_chart(p, "degrees", height = 2.4)
lor <- function(x, who) {
  x <- sort(x)
  tibble(
    share_features = c(0, seq_along(x) / length(x)),
    share_dependants = c(0, cumsum(x) / sum(x)),
    model = who
  )
}
lz <- bind_rows(
  lor(vp_feature_table(band)$required_by, "Mi Band 3 (Gini 0.65)"),
  lor(vp_feature_table(bb)$required_by, "BusyBox (Gini 0.85)")
)
p <- ggplot(lz, aes(share_features, share_dependants, colour = model)) +
  geom_abline(linetype = "dashed", colour = "#8A93A6") +
  geom_step(linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#B3261E", "#0B3D91")) +
  coord_equal() +
  labs(
    x = "share of the features, the least required first",
    y = "share of all the dependants",
    colour = NULL
  )
save_chart(p, "lorenz", width = 6.3, height = 3.2)
stopifnot(
  abs(vp_gini(vp_feature_table(band)$required_by) - 0.649) < 0.001,
  abs(vp_gini(vp_feature_table(bb)$required_by) - 0.85) < 0.001
)
xb <- sort(vp_feature_table(bb)$required_by)
fact("BusyLorenz", pct(sum(xb[seq_len(floor(0.8 * length(xb)))]) / sum(xb)))
```

![BusyBox, last month: one dot per degree. A dot at $(k, m)$ means that $m$ features are required by exactly $k$ others; both axes are logarithmic. Blue: all the dependants (complete graph); orange: the direct ones (skeleton). The blue tail is longer: a feature high in the hierarchy inherits every dependant below it.](figures/am_degrees.svg){#fig:degrees}

### The Gini coefficient: concentration in one number

**What it is.** The Gini coefficient comes from economics, where it measures how unequally income is shared in a country: 0 when everyone earns the same, close to 1 when one person earns everything. Here the "income" of a feature is the number of features that require it, and the coefficient measures how unequally the dependencies are shared among the features. Four made-up models of four features, with the `required_by` of each:

```{r gini_toy}
vp_gini(c(3, 3, 3, 3)) # every feature required by three others
vp_gini(c(0, 2, 4, 6)) # spread unevenly
vp_gini(c(0, 0, 6, 6)) # two features hold everything
vp_gini(c(0, 0, 0, 12)) # one feature holds everything
```

Equal shares give 0; the more the dependants gather on fewer features, the closer the value gets to 1. (With $k$ features the largest possible value is $(k - 1)/k$, here $3/4$: it reaches 1 only for very many features.) The total does not matter, only how it is shared: the four models have 12 dependants each.

**Why it matters.**

-   *To see whether the model rests on a few foundations.* A high Gini means that a handful of features carry most of the dependencies: changing one of them reaches much of the model, so they deserve the most careful review and the most tests, and the model is fragile where they are (see the robustness of [Robustness](patterns.html#sec:robustness)). A low Gini means that the dependencies are spread: no feature is a single point of failure, and no short list of features to watch either.

-   *To compare models of different sizes.* Two degree distributions of models with 50 and 5,000 features cannot be laid one on the other; their Gini coefficients, which are shares, can.

-   *To follow a model through time.* A Gini that grows from version to version means that new dependencies keep landing on the same features: the model is centralising around a few hubs. One number per version is easy to plot (`vp_history_apply()`, [Following a model through time](evolution.html#sec:evolution)); a chart per version is not.

-   *For any other quantity.* `vp_gini()` takes any list of numbers: `gini_excludes` in `vp_summary()` measures how unevenly the conflicts are spread, and the churn of the features over a history (`vp_feature_churn()`) can be measured the same way.

On real models:

```{r gini_models}
vp_gini(vp_feature_table(band)$required_by)
vp_gini(vp_feature_table(bb)$required_by)
```

The band, 0.65: its dependants gather on the screen and its atomic set. BusyBox, `r facts$BusyGini`: much more concentrated; most of its features are required by nobody, and a few, such as `CONFIG_FEATURE_SYSLOG`, by dozens.

**How it is computed: the Lorenz curve.** The curve answers one question for every share of the features: *the least required features, taken together, hold what share of all the dependants?* It is easiest to build it by hand on the band. Sort its features from the least to the most required, and add up as you go:

```{r lorenz_band}
vp_feature_table(band) |>
  select(feature, required_by) |>
  arrange(required_by) |>
  mutate(
    share_features = row_number() / n(),
    share_dependants = cumsum(required_by) / sum(required_by)
  )
```

Each row is one point of the curve: $x$ is `share_features`, $y$ is `share_dependants`. Read three of them:

-   *Row 5* ($x = 0.45$, $y = 0$). The five least required features, almost half of the band, are required by nothing: together they hold none of the 21 dependants. The curve runs flat along the bottom.

-   *Row 8* ($x = 0.73$, $y = 0.14$). Add three features required by one feature each: 73 % of the features now hold 3 of the 21 dependants, 14 %.

-   *Rows 9 to 11*. The last three features, the screen and its atomic set, are required by six features each. They add the remaining 18 dependants, and the curve climbs steeply to $(1, 1)$.

The *diagonal* is what the curve would be if every feature were required by as many features as every other one: then 45 % of the features would hold 45 % of the dependants, and so on. The further the curve sags below the diagonal, the more the dependants are concentrated in a few features. The Gini coefficient measures the sag: it is twice the area between the diagonal and the curve. It is 0 when the curve is the diagonal (everyone equal), and it approaches 1 when one feature holds all the dependants (the curve stays at 0 and jumps up at the last feature). The formula of `gini_required_by` in the table of `vp_summary()` above, which `vp_gini()` computes, is that area counted step by step. In the figure the curve of BusyBox stays near the bottom for longer than that of the band: the 80 % least required features of BusyBox hold only `r facts$BusyLorenz` % of all its dependants.

![Lorenz curves of `required_by`. A point $(x, y)$ means: the least required $x$ of the features, taken together, are required by $y$ of all the dependants (the sum of `required_by`). The dashed diagonal is perfect equality, every feature required as much as any other; the further a curve sags below it, the more a few features hold all the dependants. The Gini coefficient is twice the area between the diagonal and the curve.](figures/am_lorenz.svg){#fig:lorenz}

::: {.vp-box .vp-limitsbox}
**Limits: what this cannot tell you**

In the complete requires graph, a feature high in a deep hierarchy is required by everything below it, so long tails, and a high Gini, appear by construction. For the shape of what was written, use the skeleton: `vp_degree_distribution(bb, "skeleton")`, or the Gini of `direct_required_by`. The Gini says how concentrated the dependencies are, not which features hold them, nor whether that is good: a deliberate core library and an accidental bottleneck give the same number. Whether a distribution is a "power law" is a statistical question with its own test ([Is it a power law?](patterns.html#sec:powerlaw)).

:::


```{r finish, include = FALSE}
vp_finish()
vp_clean_manual_data()
```

```{r references, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
vp_references()
```
